The '''2017 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2016–17 UEFA Champions League]], the 62nd season of Europe's premier club football tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the 25th season since it was renamed from the [[European Cup]] to the [[UEFA Champions League]]. It was played at the [[Millennium Stadium]] in [[Cardiff]], Wales on 3 June 2017,<ref name="host">{{cite news |title=Cardiff to host 2017 Champions League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0250-0c510cb2a7e1-268ae41e9f4c-1000--cardiff-to-host-2017-champions-league-final/ |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=30 June 2015 |access-date=13 May 2017}}</ref> between [[Italian Football Federation|Italian]] side [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] and [[Royal Spanish Football Federation|Spanish]] side and title holders [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], in a repeat of the [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998 final]]. This was the first final to feature the title holders since [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] lost in [[2009 UEFA Champions League final|2009]]. This would also be the first European Cup final held under a closed roof.<ref name="roof">{{cite news |title=Champions League final roof closed for safety reasons |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-wales-40059676 |website=bbc.co.uk |date=26 May 2017 |access-date=27 January 2021}}</ref> Real Madrid won the match 4–1 to secure their 12th title in this competition. With this victory, Real Madrid became the first team to successfully defend their title since [[AC Milan|Milan]] in [[1990 European Cup final|1990]]. Meanwhile, Juventus lost their seventh Champions League final out of nine finals.

Real Madrid qualified as the UEFA representative at the [[2017 FIFA Club World Cup]] in the United Arab Emirates, and also earned the right to play against the winners of the [[2016–17 UEFA Europa League]], Manchester United, in the [[2017 UEFA Super Cup]], triumphing in both competitions.

==Background==
The match was a repeat of the [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998 final]], making it the eighth repeated final pairing.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0245-0e98c715cad7-29a5fa4a1a1b-1000--second-time-round-european-cup-final-rematches/ |title=Second time round: European Cup final rematches |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=16 May 2017}}</ref> Real Madrid won the 1998 final 1–0.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e96f13b027d-b6670a31954a-1000--juventus-and-real-madrid-s-previous-final-appearances/ |title=Juventus and Real Madrid's previous final appearances |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=10 May 2017}}</ref> The 2017 final was the first time since the two teams met in 1998 that both finalists had won either their domestic league or the Champions League the previous season.

[[Juventus FC|Juventus]] reached their ninth final after a 4–1 aggregate win against [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]] to maintain their undefeated record in this season's competition. Juventus also entered the final with the best defensive record in the 2016–17 Champions League, having conceded only three times.<ref>{{cite web |title=2017 UEFA Champions League Final Press Kit |url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2017/e/e_2019641_pk.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171016065719/http://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefachampionsleague/2017/e/e_2019641_pk.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=16 October 2017 |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=May 2017 |access-date=3 June 2017}}</ref> Previously Juventus won finals in [[1985 European Cup final|1985]] and [[1996 UEFA Champions League final|1996]], and lost a record six in [[1973 European Cup final|1973]], [[1983 European Cup final|1983]], [[1997 UEFA Champions League final|1997]], [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998]], [[2003 UEFA Champions League final|2003]] and [[2015 UEFA Champions League final|2015]]. This was also their 14th final in all seasonal UEFA competitions, having also played in one [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] final (winning in [[1984 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1984]]) and four [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] finals (winning in [[1977 UEFA Cup Final|1977]], [[1990 UEFA Cup Final|1990]] and [[1993 UEFA Cup Final|1993]], and losing in [[1995 UEFA Cup Final|1995]]). If they were to lose, Juventus would tie [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]]'s record of five consecutive finals lost.

Title holders [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] reached a record 15th final after a 4–2 aggregate win against [[Madrid Derby|city rivals]] [[Atlético Madrid]], knocking them out of the competition for the fourth consecutive season.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Murray |first1=Scott |title=Atlético Madrid 2–1 Real Madrid (agg 2–4): Champions League semi-final – as it happened |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2017/may/10/atletico-madrid-v-real-madrid-champions-league-semi-final-live |website=The Guardian|access-date=10 May 2017 |date=10 May 2017}}</ref> Real Madrid went into the final as top scorers of the 2016–17 Champions League, having scored 32 goals. This final was their third since 2014, and gave Real a chance to win a [[European Cup and UEFA Champions League records and statistics|record 12th title]]. Previously they won finals in [[1956 European Cup final|1956]], [[1957 European Cup final|1957]], [[1958 European Cup final|1958]], [[1959 European Cup final|1959]], [[1960 European Cup final|1960]], [[1966 European Cup final|1966]], [[1998 UEFA Champions League final|1998]], [[2000 UEFA Champions League final|2000]], [[2002 UEFA Champions League final|2002]], [[2014 UEFA Champions League final|2014]] and [[2016 UEFA Champions League final|2016]], and lost in [[1962 European Cup final|1962]], [[1964 European Cup final|1964]] and [[1981 European Cup final|1981]]. This was also their 19th final in all seasonal UEFA competitions, having also played in two [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup|Cup Winners' Cup]] finals (losing in [[1971 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1971]] and [[1983 European Cup Winners' Cup Final|1983]]) and two [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] finals (winning in [[1985 UEFA Cup Final|1985]] and [[1986 UEFA Cup Final|1986]]). Real Madrid were looking to be the first team in the Champions League era (since 1993) to win two consecutive finals. Four times prior the previous champions had advanced to the final ([[AC Milan|Milan]] in [[1995 UEFA Champions League final|1995]], [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]] in [[1996 UEFA Champions League final|1996]], Juventus in [[1997 UEFA Champions League final|1997]], and [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in [[2009 UEFA Champions League final|2009]]), but on all occasions the title holders lost.<ref>{{cite news |title=Will Madrid end Champions League holders' curse? |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e96d80bc2f6-d4e7f311245e-1000--champions-league-holders-real-madrid-still-only-back-to-ba/ |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=10 May 2017|access-date=13 May 2017}}</ref>

The two sides had previously met eighteen times in European competitions, all in the European Cup/UEFA Champions League, with a record of eight wins each and two draws. The first meeting between the two sides took place in the [[1961–62 European Cup]] quarter-finals, where Real Madrid beat Juventus 3–1 in a play-off after the two sides exchanged 1–0 away wins. The most recent meeting between the clubs had taken place in the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League]] semi-finals, where Juventus won 3–2 on aggregate to advance to the 2015 final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Juventus v Real Madrid: past meetings |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e9c7e2f3b6e-9415a00f1989-1000--juventus-v-real-madrid-past-meetings/ |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=11 May 2017 |access-date=13 May 2017}}</ref>

Juventus entered the final chasing their first [[Treble (association football)|treble]] of domestic league, domestic cup, and Champions League titles, bidding to become the ninth team to win it.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e970d07cbb3-0993516ca24c-1000--can-juventus-become-ninth-team-to-complete-treble/ |title=Can Juventus become ninth team to complete treble? |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=31 May 2017}}</ref> They won the [[2016–17 Coppa Italia]] after defeating [[SS Lazio|Lazio]] in the [[2017 Coppa Italia Final|final]] on 17 May,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e9705172235-0903917df014-1000--juventus-win-coppa-italia-madrid-on-brink-of-liga-title/ |title=Juventus win Coppa Italia, Madrid on brink of Liga title |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=17 May 2017}}</ref> and clinched the [[2016–17 Serie A]] title on 21 May.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e9c8278b075-9004183d3dc8-1000--juventus-first-to-win-six-serie-a-titles-in-a-row/ |title=Juventus first to win six Serie A titles in a row |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=21 May 2017}}</ref> Real Madrid were chasing the [[Double (association football)|double]] of domestic league and Champions League titles, having won the [[2016–17 La Liga]] on 21 May, the final day of the season.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e970d07cc44-1da7e85077d3-1000--real-madrid-end-five-year-wait-for-liga-title/ |title=Real Madrid end five-year wait for Liga title |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=21 May 2017}}</ref>

