==Pre-match==
===Ambassador===
The ambassador for the final was former Wales international [[Ian Rush]], who won the European Cup with [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] in [[1984 European Cup final|1984]], and also played for Juventus from 1987 to 1988.<ref>{{cite news |title=Ian Rush named ambassador for Cardiff final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0230-0e164fa77d1e-fc97561bd48c-1000--ian-rush-named-ambassador-for-cardiff-final/ |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=23 August 2016 |access-date=13 May 2017}}</ref>

===Identity===
[[File:Cardiff Castle (Champions League Final 2017) 35174903946 fb876f1155 o.jpg|thumb|left|upright|The dragon logo on Cardiff Castle]]

UEFA unveiled the brand identity of the final on 25 August 2016 in [[Monaco]] during the group stage draw.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cardiff 2017 final identity launched |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0230-0e94cab78cd6-3e902dfc0305-1000--cardiff-2017-final-identity-launched/ |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=25 August 2016 |access-date=13 May 2017}}</ref>

===Ticketing===
With a stadium capacity of 66,000 for the final, a total of 41,500 tickets were available to fans and the general public; the two finalist teams received 18,000 tickets each and 5,500 tickets were made available for sale to fans worldwide via ''UEFA.com'' from 17 to 28 March 2017 in four price categories: £390, £275, £140, and £60. The remaining tickets were allocated to the local organising committee, UEFA and national associations, commercial partners and broadcasters, and to serve the corporate hospitality programme.<ref>{{cite web |title=2017 UEFA Champions League final ticket sales launched |url=https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=2449749.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170710002554/http://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=2449749.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=10 July 2017 |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=17 March 2017}}</ref>

===Officials===
In May 2017, German [[referee (association football)|referee]] [[Felix Brych]] was selected to supervise the final. He was joined by fellow German officials Mark Borsch and Stefan Lupp as [[assistant referee (association football)|assistant referees]], [[Bastian Dankert]] and [[Marco Fritz]] as [[assistant referee (association football)#Additional assistant referee|additional assistant referees]], [[Rafael Foltyn]] as [[assistant referee (association football)#Fifth official and reserve assistant referee|reserve assistant referee]], and Serbian [[Milorad Mažić]] as [[assistant referee (association football)#Fourth official|fourth official]].<ref name="referee">{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e96fa086809-5d3e29924dd6-1000--felix-brych-appointed-champions-league-final-referee/|publisher=Union of European Football Associations |title=Felix Brych appointed Champions League final referee |date=12 May 2017}}</ref>

===Opening ceremony===
[[File:UEFA Champions League Final Cardiff 2017.jpg|thumb|The opening ceremony]]

American hip-hop group [[The Black Eyed Peas]] performed at the [[opening ceremony]], which included a pyrotechnic display that ran over time and delayed the kick-off for several minutes.<ref>{{cite news |last=Gibson |first=Sean |date=3 June 2017 |title=Fans not happy as Champions League final kick-off delayed after 'awful' Black Eyed Peas performance |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2017/06/03/fans-not-happy-champions-league-final-kick-off-delayed-awful/ |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220112/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2017/06/03/fans-not-happy-champions-league-final-kick-off-delayed-awful/ |archive-date=12 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |work=[[Telegraph Media Group|The Telegraph]] |access-date=3 June 2017}}{{cbignore}}</ref> The [[UEFA Champions League Anthem]] featured a recording of Italian tenor [[Andrea Bocelli]] from the previous season's final.<ref>{{cite tweet |number=871076655892180992 |user=beinsports_FR |title=🏆 ⚽️ #JUVRMA L'hymne de la Ligue des... |date=3 June 2017}}</ref>

===Related events===
The [[2017 UEFA Women's Champions League final]] was held two days prior, on 1 June 2017, at the [[Cardiff City Stadium]], with title holders [[Olympique Lyonnais (Women)|Lyon]] beating [[Paris Saint-Germain Féminines|Paris Saint-Germain]] 7–6 on penalties, following a 0–0 draw after extra time. The winning penalty was scored by Lyon goalkeeper [[Sarah Bouhaddi]], immediately after her opposite number, [[Katarzyna Kiedrzynek]], had missed her kick.

The annual UEFA Champions Festival was held between 1–4 June 2017 at [[Cardiff Bay]].<ref>{{cite web |title=UEFA CHAMPIONS FESTIVAL |url=http://www.faw.cymru/cardiff2017/whatson/uefa-champions-festival/ |website=faw.cymru |publisher=Football Association of Wales |access-date=13 May 2017 |archive-date=19 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230419163637/https://www.faw.cymru/cardiff2017/whatson/uefa-champions-festival/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
[[File:Cristiano Ronaldo entrenando (crop) (cropped).jpg|thumb|[[Cristiano Ronaldo]], who scored twice, was named [[Man of the Match]].]]

Juventus dominated possession in the opening 19 minutes of the match, and had three major chances to score. [[Gonzalo Higuaín]] had two shots on goal that were easily saved by [[Keylor Navas]], while [[Miralem Pjanić]] had a more dangerous chance that Navas dived to push wide to his right. Despite the scoring chances, as well as the closing down of Real Madrid's counterattacks in the midfield, [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] scored the first goal of the match in the 20th minute; [[Dani Carvajal]] played a one-two on the right with Ronaldo before passing across for him to shoot low to the left corner of the net, which deflected in off the right foot of [[Leonardo Bonucci]].<ref name="NYTimes">{{cite news |last1=Das |first1=Andrew |last2=Smith |first2=Rory |date=3 June 2017 |title=Real Madrid Beats Juventus for 2nd Straight Champions League Title |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/06/03/sports/champions-league-final-score.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=3 June 2017}}</ref><ref name="Guardian">{{cite news |date=3 June 2017 |title=Real Madrid and Cristiano Ronaldo see off Juventus to win Champions League |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/jun/03/juventus-real-madrid-champions-league-final-match-report |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=3 June 2017}}</ref> Juventus responded with a goal seven minutes later to bring the score to 1–1. [[Mario Mandžukić]] chested down a ball from Higuaín and hooked the ball over his shoulder from {{convert|15|yd|m}},<ref name="NYTimes"/><ref>{{cite news |last=Joseph |first=Adi |date=3 June 2017 |title=Juventus' Mario Mandzukic nets one of the craziest goals in Champions League history |url=https://ftw.usatoday.com/2017/06/mario-mandzukic-bicycle-kick-goal-juventus-vs-real-madrid |work=[[USA Today]] |access-date=3 June 2017}}</ref> which arced over Navas. The goal was heralded as one of the best goals ever scored in a Champions League final and was compared to [[Zinedine Zidane]]'s goal in the [[2002 UEFA Champions League final|2002 final]] for Real Madrid.<ref>{{cite news |date=3 June 2017 |title=Is Mario Mandzukic's goal the best ever in a Champions League final? |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/mario-mandzukic-goal-champions-league-final-juventus-real-madrid-a7771516.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220501/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/european/mario-mandzukic-goal-champions-league-final-juventus-real-madrid-a7771516.html |archive-date=1 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |work=[[The Independent]] |access-date=3 June 2017}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Juventus remained in control through the end of the first half, while Real Madrid conceded two yellow cards for defenders [[Sergio Ramos]] and [[Dani Carvajal]].<ref name="NYTimes"/><ref name="Guardian"/>

Real Madrid began the second half strongly and in control,<ref name="Guardian"/> though play was stopped several times for fouls and injuries to both sides.<ref name="Guardian-Live">{{cite news |last=Glendenning |first=Barry |date=3 June 2017 |title=Juventus 1–4 Real Madrid: Champions League final 2017 – live! |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2017/jun/03/juventus-v-real-madrid-champions-league-final-2017-live |work=The Guardian |access-date=3 June 2017}}</ref> Real Madrid took a 2–1 lead in the 61st minute from a long-range strike from [[Casemiro]], which deflected off [[Sami Khedira]] and past [[Gianluigi Buffon]] to the left corner of the net.<ref name="NYTimes"/><ref name="Guardian-Live"/> Three minutes later, Ronaldo scored Madrid's third goal of the night and his second, finishing in the {{convert|6|yd|m|adj=mid}} box from a cut-back from [[Luka Modrić]] on the right.<ref name="NYTimes"/> Juventus, fending off additional attacks from Madrid, picked up three additional yellow cards, including one to newly substituted [[Juan Cuadrado]].<ref name="Guardian-Live"/> Real Madrid replaced striker [[Karim Benzema]] with [[Gareth Bale]], a [[Cardiff]] native who had not been fit enough to start but was given a homecoming.<ref name="Guardian"/> Cuadrado picked up a second yellow card and was sent off in the 84th minute for a shove on [[Sergio Ramos]], who went down while holding his foot.<ref name="Guardian-Live"/> [[Marco Asensio]] scored Madrid's fourth and final goal in the 90th minute, a tap-in from {{convert|12|yd|m}} after a cut-back from [[Marcelo (footballer, born 1988)|Marcelo]] from the left.<ref name="Guardian-Live"/>

